**Read:** [Material families and mechanical response — Cambridge DoITPoMS](../sources/Material-Families-and-Mechanical-Response-DoITPoMS.lingua#r_79PNN694CQ82ENKZC6STQJ5KN9), Brittle Fracture and Toughening; [Polymer Physics — MIT 3.063](../sources/Polymer-Physics-MIT-3-063.lingua#r_5EPM1GX0YZDKN71N771PQCWQ5P), mechanical properties; [Modules in Mechanics of Materials — David Roylance](../../mechanical-engineering-and-machine-design/sources/Mechanics-of-Materials-Roylance.lingua#r_7K2RYA9504FGFGJTVWHMWXD5WY), Fatigue and Engineering Viscoelasticity; [Applied Mechanics of Solids — Allan F. Bower](../../mechanical-engineering-and-machine-design/sources/Applied-Mechanics-of-Solids-Bower.lingua#r_109WFZBWMEMGHV9TGFD22H2F1W), interface fracture. Extend these readings through a complete primary fatigue study for each chosen family.

In a polymer, cyclic hysteresis can generate heat, altering the response that produced the heat. In a brittle ceramic, time-dependent environmental crack growth and cycle-dependent degradation of shielding may coexist. In a laminate, matrix cracking and delamination can reduce stiffness before final fibre failure.

Separate an imposed cycle from the actual local mechanical and thermal history. Damage indicators such as stiffness loss, acoustic signals or visible cracks need an explicit relation to the failure question. The same scalar 'damage' need not represent the same physical state in different models.

**Work:** Compare constant-frequency and equal-duration experiments to separate time and cycle effects. Track a complete crack or stiffness history, retaining interruption and conditioning details. Explain why borrowing a metallic Paris-law fit or a linear cumulative-damage rule requires new evidence in the chosen polymer or composite.

**Return:** [Materials engineering — structure, processing and useful behavior](../Materials-engineering.lingua#r_05BF5JM8141RKF1VT5998YFZQ9).
